  "raw": " required to go on foot. He achieved a position as tutor at Sketton College; this post gave him leisure time to write several controversial essays concerning the operation of the French Revolutionary Constitution of 1793–1799. Somewhat bitterly, he accused France of trying to prevent all progress by its revolutionary leaders, expressing particular alarm over the activities of Napoleon Bonaparte. Three years later, Burke enjoyed a brief crisis of health whilst working part-time on an agricultural scheme near Lindsey in Lindseyshire and lived on food given to him without clearing it (and has been blamed for amassing some £2 () with which he occasionally spent the night). On 19 January 1801, he was elected Member of Parliament for Norma but although formally given at least 14 days notice before the Kingdom of Sicily threatened to impose war against Great Britain, he refused to leave parliament until Minister Joseph Travaille took effectively charge and informed the House that military operations were \"in full swing\". His portrayal of Lord Cornwall",
